    "lem-pac-bayes-donsker-varadhan-inequality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-pac-bayes-prior-and-posterior",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ be a measurable space. A **prior** is a probability measure $\\pi$ on $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ fixed independently of the training sample. A **posterior** relative to $\\pi$ is a probability measure $\\rho$ on $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ that is absolutely continuous with respect to $\\pi$, meaning that $\\pi(A)=0$ implies $\\rho(A)=0$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal A$.\n\nFor such $\\rho$ and $\\pi$, define their **Kullback–Leibler relative entropy** by\n$$\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi):=\\sup_{\\mathcal P}\\sum_{A\\in\\mathcal P}\\rho(A)\\log\\frac{\\rho(A)}{\\pi(A)},$$\nwhere the supremum is over all finite measurable partitions $\\mathcal P$ of $\\Theta$. A term with $\\rho(A)=0$ is $0$; a term with $\\rho(A)>0=\\pi(A)$ is $+\\infty$. The value may be $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-it-finite-log-sum-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(a_i)_{i=1}^n,(b_i)_{i=1}^n$ be finite nonnegative real vectors, with $A=\\sum_i a_i$ and $B=\\sum_i b_i$. Interpret $\\sum_{a_i>0}a_i\\ln(a_i/b_i)$ as $+\\infty$ if some $a_i>0=b_i$; otherwise zero coordinates contribute $0$. Interpret $A\\ln(A/B)$ as $0$ if $A=0$ and as $+\\infty$ if $A>0=B$. Then\n\n$$\\sum_{a_i>0}a_i\\ln\\frac{a_i}{b_i}\\ge A\\ln\\frac AB.$$\n\nWhen $A,B>0$, equality holds exactly when $a_i=(A/B)b_i$ for every $i$. If $A>0=B$, both sides are $+\\infty$ and no finite equality characterization is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in The exponential series converges absolutely for every real argument. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of The factorial $n!$ and the falling factorial $n^{\\underline{k}}$, defined by recursion in $\\mathbb{N}$, $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image (The canonical natural $\\iota(n) = n \\cdot 1_F$ of a field, Canonical naturals are positive and strictly increasing), and powers and series are those of Integer powers $a^m$ and Series, partial sums, convergence and the sum, divergence, and the tail series.\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ (A real power series about a centre, its interval of convergence, and its radius in $[0,+\\infty]$). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because The exponential is a continuous bijection from $\\mathbb{R}$ onto $(0,\\infty)$ states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, $\\exp(x)>0$ and\n$$\\exp(-x)=\\frac1{\\exp(x)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The exponential function is continuous and strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\exp(x+y)=\\exp(x)\\exp(y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ (Measures on sigma-algebras). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** (Measure spaces); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space.\n\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is measurable, its **expectation** is the\n  extended-valued integral\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ or $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C$ is integrable in the sense\n  of Integrable real and complex functions, and their integrals, its\n  **expectation** is again\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P,$$\n  now a finite real or complex number.\n\nFor an integrable real random variable,\n$$\\mathbb E[X]=\\mathbb E[X^+]-\\mathbb E[X^-],$$\nwhere $X^+$ and $X^-$ are its positive and negative parts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable. Its positive and negative parts are\n$$f^+:=\\max\\{f,0\\},\\qquad f^-:=\\max\\{-f,0\\};$$\nthey are measurable by\nClosure properties of measurable functions used by the integral and\nsatisfy $f=f^+-f^-$ and $|f|=f^++f^-$.\n\nThe **Lebesgue integral of a real measurable function** is defined whenever at\nmost one of $\\int f^+\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int f^-\\,d\\mu$ is $+\\infty$, in which case\n$$\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\int f^+\\,d\\mu-\\int f^-\\,d\\mu.$$\nThe function is **integrable** when both integrals are finite, equivalently\nwhen $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$.\n\nFor a complex measurable function $h=u+iv$ with\n$u=\\operatorname{Re}h,\\ v=\\operatorname{Im}h$\n(Real and imaginary parts, complex conjugation, and modulus), define $h$ to be\n**integrable** when $|h|$ is integrable, and then define\n$$\\int h\\,d\\mu:=\\int u\\,d\\mu+i\\int v\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable\n(Extended-real-valued measurable functions). Its\n**nonnegative Lebesgue integral** is\n$$\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\left\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu:\\ s \\text{ is nonnegative simple and } 0\\le s\\le f\\right\\},$$\nwhere the simple integral on the right is the one from\nThe integral of a nonnegative simple function.\n\nThe set of admissible simple minorants is nonempty because it contains the zero\nfunction, which is simple (Nonnegative simple measurable functions).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-nonnegative-simple-measurable-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space. A measurable function\n$s:X\\to[0,+\\infty)\\subseteq\\overline{\\mathbb R}$\n(Extended-real-valued measurable functions) is a\n**nonnegative simple measurable function** when it has finite range.\n\nEquivalently, there are pairwise disjoint measurable sets\n$E_1,\\dots,E_m\\in\\mathcal A$ and coefficients $c_1,\\dots,c_m\\in[0,+\\infty)$\nsuch that\n$$s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\chi_{E_j}.$$\nAny such display is a **simple representation** of $s$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\chi_{E_j}$ be a simple representation of a\nnonnegative simple measurable function\n(Nonnegative simple measurable functions) on a measure space\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$, so the $E_j\\in\\mathcal A$ are pairwise disjoint and\n$c_j\\ge0$. Its\n**simple integral** is\n$$\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mu(E_j),$$\nwhere $\\mu$ is the given measure (Measures on sigma-algebras) and the convention\n$0\\cdot(+\\infty)=0$ is fixed once and for all.\n\nThe next lemma proves that this value is independent of the chosen simple\nrepresentation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. Let $f,g:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable and let $c\\ge0$.\n\n1. If $f\\le g$, then $\\int f\\,d\\mu\\le\\int g\\,d\\mu$.\n2. If $c>0$, then $\\int cf\\,d\\mu=c\\int f\\,d\\mu$. For $c=0$,\n   the integral of the zero function is $0$. Neither clause forms the\n   undefined extended-real product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$ and $(Y,\\mathcal{B})$ be measurable spaces\n(Measurable spaces and measurable sets). A function $f : X \\to Y$ is **measurable** when\n\n$$f^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{A} \\qquad \\text{for every } B \\in \\mathcal{B}.$$\n\nEquivalently, the family\n\n$$\\mathcal{C}_f := \\{\\, B \\subseteq Y : f^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{A} \\,\\}$$\n\nis a sigma-algebra on $Y$ containing $\\mathcal{B}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f:X\\to Y$ is continuous between topological spaces, then\n$f^{-1}[B]\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ for every $B\\in\\mathcal B(Y)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-composition-with-borel-functions-preserves-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$, $(Y,\\mathcal{B})$, and $(Z,\\mathcal{C})$ be measurable\nspaces. If $f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Y,\\mathcal{B})$ is measurable and\n$g : (Y,\\mathcal{B}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable, then\n$g \\circ f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable.\n\nIn particular, if $f$ is measurable and $g$ is a Borel measurable function on\nits codomain, then $g \\circ f$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
