    "thm-pac-bayes-kl-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a supervised prediction problem with joint law $P$, the **population risk** of any measurable predictor $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$ (in particular, every $f\\in\\mathcal F$) is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(f):=\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}\\ell(f(x),y)\\,P(d(x,y))\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor an ordered sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_m,y_m))$ of length $m\\ge1$, the **empirical risk** or **training risk** of any such $f$ is\n$$\\widehat R_S(f):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(f(x_i),y_i)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nNeither definition assumes finite risk. The sum uses extended nonnegative arithmetic; since $m\\ge1$, any summand equal to $+\\infty$ makes the empirical risk $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-population-and-empirical-risk-are-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In a supervised prediction problem, for every measurable predictor $f$, the function $(x,y)\\mapsto\\ell(f(x),y)$ is measurable, so its population risk is a defined extended nonnegative integral. For every sample size $m\\ge1$, the empirical risk $S\\mapsto\\widehat R_S(f)$ is an extended nonnegative measurable function on the finite product sample space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in Law or distribution of a random element agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in Independent random elements. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-measurable-functions-preserve-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an independent family of random elements\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$. For each $i$, let\n$g_i:(S_i,\\Sigma_i)\\to(T_i,\\mathcal T_i)$ be measurable. Then the family\n$(g_i\\circ X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is independent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-change-of-variables-for-expectation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S,\\Sigma)$ be a random element, let\n$\\mathbb P_X$ be its law, and let $g:(S,\\Sigma)\\to\\mathbb R$ or\n$g:(S,\\Sigma)\\to\\mathbb C$ be measurable.\n\n1. If $g\\ge0$, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[g(X)]=\\int_S g\\,d\\mathbb P_X.$$\n2. If $g(X)$ is integrable, then $g$ is integrable with respect to\n   $\\mathbb P_X$ and the same formula holds:\n   $$\\mathbb E[g(X)]=\\int_S g\\,d\\mathbb P_X.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-bounded-variable-hoeffding-mgf",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Y$ be a measurable real random variable on a probability space with $0\\le Y\\le1$ almost surely. Then, for every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$\\mathbb E\\exp\\{\\lambda(Y-\\mathbb EY)\\}\\le \\exp(\\lambda^2/8).$$\n\nThis proof uses only finite product and scalar integral arguments; it does not assume the Axiom of Choice.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-factorization-of-expectations-for-independent-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}$ be independent real random variables on a\ncommon probability space, and let $g_i:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be Borel\nmeasurable for each $i<n$.\n\n1. If every $g_i$ is nonnegative, then\n   $$\\mathbb E\\left[\\prod_{i<n}g_i(X_i)\\right]=\\prod_{i<n}\\mathbb E[g_i(X_i)]$$\n   in $[0,+\\infty]$. In this finite product of expectations, a zero factor\n   makes the product zero even when another factor is $+\\infty$; otherwise\n   multiplication has its usual extended nonnegative meaning.\n2. If every $g_i(X_i)$ is integrable, then $\\prod_{i<n}g_i(X_i)$ is integrable\n   and the same factorization holds in $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\exp(x+y)=\\exp(x)\\exp(y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ (Measures on sigma-algebras). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** (Measure spaces); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B)$ be measurable spaces. Their\n**product sigma-algebra** is\n\n$$\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B := \\sigma(\\{A \\times B : A \\in \\mathcal A,\\ B \\in \\mathcal B\\}),$$\n\nthe sigma-algebra on $X \\times Y$ generated by the measurable rectangles of\nMeasurable rectangles in a product of measurable spaces.\n\nFor the empty family, put $\\prod_{i<0}X_i:=\\{()\\}$ and define its **empty\nproduct sigma-algebra** by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<0}\\mathcal A_i:=\\{\\varnothing,\\{()\\}\\}.$$\n\nFor a nonempty finite family of measurable spaces $(X_i,\\mathcal A_i)$ with\n$i<n$, the **finite product sigma-algebra** is defined recursively by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<1}\\mathcal A_i := \\mathcal A_0,\\qquad \\bigotimes_{i<n+1}\\mathcal A_i := \\left(\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathcal A_i\\right)\\otimes\\mathcal A_n.$$\n\nThis recursive definition is the finite base case used later when the library\npasses to countable product constructions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. Then:\n\n1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   The product measure of two sigma-finite measure spaces is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "cor-markov-inequality-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is a nonnegative random variable on a probability\nspace and $a>0$, then\n$$\\mathbb P(X\\ge a)\\le\\frac{\\mathbb E[X]}{a}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-pac-bayes-donsker-varadhan-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ be a measurable space, let $\\pi$ be a probability measure, and let $\\rho$ be a probability measure absolutely continuous with respect to $\\pi$, meaning that $\\pi(A)=0$ implies $\\rho(A)=0$ for all $A\\in\\mathcal A$. Define\n$$\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi):=\\sup_{\\mathcal P}\\sum_{A\\in\\mathcal P}\\rho(A)\\log\\frac{\\rho(A)}{\\pi(A)},$$\nwhere $\\mathcal P$ ranges over finite measurable partitions of $\\Theta$, a zero-numerator term is $0$, and a positive numerator over a zero denominator is $+\\infty$. For every bounded measurable $f:\\Theta\\to\\mathbb R$,\n$$\\mathbb E_{\\theta\\sim\\rho}f(\\theta)\\le \\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi)+\\log\\mathbb E_{\\theta\\sim\\pi}\\exp(f(\\theta)),$$\nwith the inequality interpreted as vacuous when $\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi)=+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-arithmetic-and-lattice-operations-preserve-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ be a measurable space and let\n$f,g:X\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be measurable for the Borel sigma-algebra on\nthe extended real line. Products of functions use\n$\\odot_X$ from A total pointwise product for extended-real-valued functions, and $cf$\nabbreviates $\\underline c\\odot_X f$ for the constant function $\\underline c$.\nThe magnitude function is $|f|=\\max(f,-f)$ as in\nThe positive and negative parts of a function. Then:\n\n1. $cf$ is measurable for every real scalar $c$;\n2. $\\max(f,g)$, $\\min(f,g)$, $|f|$, $f^+$, and $f^-$ are measurable;\n3. if the partial pointwise sum $f+g$ is defined at every point, then it is measurable;\n4. the pointwise product $fg:=f\\odot_X g$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-composition-with-borel-functions-preserves-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$, $(Y,\\mathcal{B})$, and $(Z,\\mathcal{C})$ be measurable\nspaces. If $f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Y,\\mathcal{B})$ is measurable and\n$g : (Y,\\mathcal{B}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable, then\n$g \\circ f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable.\n\nIn particular, if $f$ is measurable and $g$ is a Borel measurable function on\nits codomain, then $g \\circ f$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because The exponential is a continuous bijection from $\\mathbb{R}$ onto $(0,\\infty)$ states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "def-pac-bayes-prior-and-posterior",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ be a measurable space. A **prior** is a probability measure $\\pi$ on $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ fixed independently of the training sample. A **posterior** relative to $\\pi$ is a probability measure $\\rho$ on $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ that is absolutely continuous with respect to $\\pi$, meaning that $\\pi(A)=0$ implies $\\rho(A)=0$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal A$.\n\nFor such $\\rho$ and $\\pi$, define their **Kullback–Leibler relative entropy** by\n$$\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi):=\\sup_{\\mathcal P}\\sum_{A\\in\\mathcal P}\\rho(A)\\log\\frac{\\rho(A)}{\\pi(A)},$$\nwhere the supremum is over all finite measurable partitions $\\mathcal P$ of $\\Theta$. A term with $\\rho(A)=0$ is $0$; a term with $\\rho(A)>0=\\pi(A)$ is $+\\infty$. The value may be $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in The exponential series converges absolutely for every real argument. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of The factorial $n!$ and the falling factorial $n^{\\underline{k}}$, defined by recursion in $\\mathbb{N}$, $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image (The canonical natural $\\iota(n) = n \\cdot 1_F$ of a field, Canonical naturals are positive and strictly increasing), and powers and series are those of Integer powers $a^m$ and Series, partial sums, convergence and the sum, divergence, and the tail series.\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ (A real power series about a centre, its interval of convergence, and its radius in $[0,+\\infty]$). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, $\\exp(x)>0$ and\n$$\\exp(-x)=\\frac1{\\exp(x)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The exponential function is continuous and strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For fixed $\\theta$, the map $z\\mapsto(\\theta,z)$ is measurable: the preimage of a generating rectangle $A\\times B$ is $B$ if $\\theta\\in A$, and empty otherwise. Thus $f_\\theta(z):=\\ell(\\theta,z)$ is measurable. Apply [F1] to the supervised problem with instance space $\\mathcal Z$, singleton label space $\\{*\\}$, action space $[0,C]$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, predictor class $\\{f_\\theta\\}$, loss $L(a,*)=a$, and joint law $D\\otimes\\delta_*$, where $\\delta_*(\\{*\\})=1$. Tonelli [F8] identifies its population risk with $R(\\theta)$, and its empirical risk on $((Z_i,*))_{i=1}^m$ is $\\widehat R_S(\\theta)$. If $C=0$, both risks are zero and the bound follows because [F13] gives $\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi)\\ge0$ and [F12] gives $\\log(1/\\delta)>0$. Suppose $C>0$ and put $Y_i=\\ell(\\theta,Z_i)$. By [F2]–[F4], the $Y_i$ are independent and $\\mathbb EY_i=R(\\theta)$. Apply [F6] to $Y_i/C\\in[0,1]$ with $t=-\\lambda C/m$, and use [F5] to rescale the centered variable, obtaining $\\mathbb E\\exp(-\\lambda(Y_i-R(\\theta))/m)\\le\\exp(\\lambda^2C^2/(8m^2))$. By [F7] and the exponential addition formula, the finite product factors, so $\\mathbb E_S\\exp\\big(\\lambda(R(\\theta)-\\widehat R_S(\\theta))\\big)\\le\\exp\\left(\\frac{\\lambda^2C^2}{8m}\\right)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F12",
            "F13",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The fixed-predictor risks are defined by [F1], and the bounded product-measurable loss makes $R(\\theta)$ measurable in $\\theta$ by [F8]; finite projection, arithmetic, and Borel composition make $(\\theta,S)\\mapsto\\exp(\\lambda(R(\\theta)-\\widehat R_S(\\theta)))$ product-measurable by [F11]. By [F14], this integrand lies between $e^{-\\lambda C}>0$ and $e^{\\lambda C}$. Thus $M_\\lambda(S):=\\mathbb E_{\\theta\\sim\\pi}\\exp(\\lambda(R(\\theta)-\\widehat R_S(\\theta)))$ is measurable, positive, and Tonelli [F8] exchanges the prior and sample integrals. Integrating step 1.1 over $\\pi$ gives $\\mathbb E_S M_\\lambda(S)\\le\\exp(\\lambda^2C^2/(8m))$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F8",
            "F11",
            "F14",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "By [F9] with threshold $\\exp(\\lambda^2C^2/(8m))/\\delta$, the event $M_\\lambda(S)\\le\\exp(\\lambda^2C^2/(8m))/\\delta$ has probability at least $1-\\delta$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F9",
            "F14",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "On this event, for every posterior $\\rho\\ll\\pi$, apply [F10] to the bounded measurable function $f_S(\\theta)=\\lambda(R(\\theta)-\\widehat R_S(\\theta))$. This gives $\\lambda(\\mathbb E_\\rho R-\\mathbb E_\\rho\\widehat R_S)\\le\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi)+\\log M_\\lambda(S)\\le\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi)+\\lambda^2C^2/(8m)+\\log(1/\\delta)$ by [F12]. Divide by $\\lambda>0$; when the KL value is infinite the inequality is vacuous.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F10",
            "F12",
            "F14",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "The event in step 3.1 is independent of $\\rho$, and step 4.1 proves the bound for every posterior on that one event, which has probability at least $1-\\delta$. The $C=0$ case was handled in step 1.1, so the theorem holds for all $C\\ge0$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "given"
          ]
        }
