    "cor-compression-sample-size-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-compression-generalization-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma)$ be a measurable space, let $\\mathcal H$ be a nonempty class of measurable maps $c:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$, fix a target $c\\in\\mathcal H$, and let $D$ be a probability measure on $\\mathcal X$. Put $\\mathcal Z=\\mathcal X\\times\\{0,1\\}$ with the product sigma-algebra. Let $m,d\\in\\mathbb N$ satisfy $m>d$, set $[m]=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$, and draw $(X_1,\\ldots,X_m)$ under $D^m$, labeling $X_j$ by $c(X_j)$. Write $S=((X_1,c(X_1)),\\ldots,(X_m,c(X_m)))$. For each $0\\le i\\le d$, let $G_i:\\mathcal Z^i\\times\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$ be a fixed jointly measurable reconstruction map (for $i=0$, $G_0$ is measurable on $\\mathcal X$). For $I=\\{i_1<\\cdots<i_k\\}\\subseteq[m]$, define $S_I=((X_{i_1},c(X_{i_1})),\\ldots,(X_{i_k},c(X_{i_k})))$ and $g(S_I)(x):=G_k(S_I,x)$; for $I=\\varnothing$, let $S_I$ be the empty sequence and use $G_0$. Define $R_D(g(S_I)):=D\\{x:g(S_I)(x)\\ne c(x)\\}$. For every $\\epsilon\\in(0,1)$,\n$$\\Pr_{D^m}\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}\\text{there is an index subset }I\\subseteq[m],\\ |I|\\le d,\\text{ such that }R_D(g(S_I))>\\epsilon\\\\[-2pt]\\text{and }g(S_I)(X_j)=c(X_j)\\text{ for every }j\\in[m]\\end{array}\\right\\}\\le\\sum_{i=0}^{d}\\binom{m}{i}(1-\\epsilon)^{m-i}.$$\nThe event displayed above is measurable. Thus any measurable consistent compression learner of size at most $d$ using these reconstructors has failure probability no larger than this bound.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-compression-binomial-sum-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For natural numbers $m,d$ with $1\\le d\\le m$,\n$$\\sum_{i=0}^{d}\\binom{m}{i}\\le\\left(\\frac{e\\,m}{d}\\right)^d,$$\nwhere $\\binom{m}{i}$ is the number of $i$-element subsets of an $m$-element set, natural numbers and binomial counts in real expressions denote their canonical real images, and $e=\\exp(1)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-exponential-dominates-one-plus-x",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$1+x\\le\\exp(x).$$\nConsequently, if $0\\le p\\le1$ and $m\\in\\mathbb N$, then\n$$ (1-p)^m\\le\\exp(-mp). $$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because The exponential is a continuous bijection from $\\mathbb{R}$ onto $(0,\\infty)$ states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in The exponential series converges absolutely for every real argument. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of The factorial $n!$ and the falling factorial $n^{\\underline{k}}$, defined by recursion in $\\mathbb{N}$, $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image (The canonical natural $\\iota(n) = n \\cdot 1_F$ of a field, Canonical naturals are positive and strictly increasing), and powers and series are those of Integer powers $a^m$ and Series, partial sums, convergence and the sum, divergence, and the tail series.\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ (A real power series about a centre, its interval of convergence, and its radius in $[0,+\\infty]$). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\exp(x+y)=\\exp(x)\\exp(y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The exponential function is continuous and strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
