          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ (Measures on sigma-algebras). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** (Measure spaces); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a supervised prediction problem with joint law $P$, the **population risk** of any measurable predictor $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$ (in particular, every $f\\in\\mathcal F$) is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(f):=\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}\\ell(f(x),y)\\,P(d(x,y))\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor an ordered sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_m,y_m))$ of length $m\\ge1$, the **empirical risk** or **training risk** of any such $f$ is\n$$\\widehat R_S(f):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(f(x_i),y_i)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nNeither definition assumes finite risk. The sum uses extended nonnegative arithmetic; since $m\\ge1$, any summand equal to $+\\infty$ makes the empirical risk $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-population-and-empirical-risk-are-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In a supervised prediction problem, for every measurable predictor $f$, the function $(x,y)\\mapsto\\ell(f(x),y)$ is measurable, so its population risk is a defined extended nonnegative integral. For every sample size $m\\ge1$, the empirical risk $S\\mapsto\\widehat R_S(f)$ is an extended nonnegative measurable function on the finite product sample space.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-pac-bayes-prior-and-posterior",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ be a measurable space. A **prior** is a probability measure $\\pi$ on $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ fixed independently of the training sample. A **posterior** relative to $\\pi$ is a probability measure $\\rho$ on $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ that is absolutely continuous with respect to $\\pi$, meaning that $\\pi(A)=0$ implies $\\rho(A)=0$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal A$.\n\nFor such $\\rho$ and $\\pi$, define their **Kullback–Leibler relative entropy** by\n$$\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi):=\\sup_{\\mathcal P}\\sum_{A\\in\\mathcal P}\\rho(A)\\log\\frac{\\rho(A)}{\\pi(A)},$$\nwhere the supremum is over all finite measurable partitions $\\mathcal P$ of $\\Theta$. A term with $\\rho(A)=0$ is $0$; a term with $\\rho(A)>0=\\pi(A)$ is $+\\infty$. The value may be $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because The exponential is a continuous bijection from $\\mathbb{R}$ onto $(0,\\infty)$ states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-pac-bayes-kl-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A)$ and $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal B)$ be measurable spaces. Let $\\pi$ and $D$ be probability measures on them, respectively. Let $C\\ge0$ and let $\\ell:\\Theta\\times\\mathcal Z\\to[0,C]$ be measurable with respect to $\\mathcal A\\otimes\\mathcal B$. Let $m\\ge1$ and let $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ consist of independent observations with common law $D$, equivalently having product law $D^m$. Define\n$$R(\\theta):=\\int_{\\mathcal Z}\\ell(\\theta,z)D(dz),\\qquad \\widehat R_S(\\theta):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(\\theta,Z_i).$$\nFix the prior $\\pi$ independently of $S$, and fix $\\lambda>0$ and $\\delta\\in(0,1)$. For a probability measure $\\rho$ with $\\rho\\ll\\pi$ (that is, $\\pi(A)=0$ implies $\\rho(A)=0$ for all measurable $A$), define\n$$\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi):=\\sup_{\\mathcal P}\\sum_{A\\in\\mathcal P}\\rho(A)\\log\\frac{\\rho(A)}{\\pi(A)},$$\nwhere $\\mathcal P$ ranges over finite measurable partitions of $\\Theta$, terms with zero numerator are $0$, and a positive numerator over zero denominator is $+\\infty$. With probability at least $1-\\delta$ over $S$, simultaneously for every such posterior $\\rho$,\n$$\\mathbb E_{\\theta\\sim\\rho}R(\\theta)\\le \\mathbb E_{\\theta\\sim\\rho}\\widehat R_S(\\theta)+\\frac{\\lambda C^2}{8m}+\\frac{\\mathrm{KL}(\\rho\\|\\pi)+\\log(1/\\delta)}{\\lambda}.$$\nAn infinite relative entropy makes the bound vacuous. The conclusion bounds posterior-average risk, not the risk of every posterior draw.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-natural-logarithm-bounded-by-argument",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x\\ge1$, the natural logarithm of\nThe natural logarithm as the inverse of the exponential function satisfies\n$$0\\le\\log x\\le x-1\\le x.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "sample-selected-prior",
          "claim": "For each sample $s=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{10})$, its observed support has at most ten points, so its complement in $\\mathcal X$ has at least ten points. Let $A(s)$ be the lexicographically first ten-element subset of that complement. This defines a measurable function of the sample because the sample space is finite. Put $\\pi_s=\\rho_s=\\delta_{h_{A(s)}}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
