    "ex-finite-support-sample-compression": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-sample-compression-scheme",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma)$ be a measurable instance space, let $\\{0,1\\}$ have its full discrete sigma-algebra, and give $\\mathcal Z:=\\mathcal X\\times\\{0,1\\}$ the product sigma-algebra. For each $k\\in\\mathbb N$, give $\\mathcal Z^k$ its finite product sigma-algebra, with $\\mathcal Z^0=\\{()\\}$. Let $\\mathcal H$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $c:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$. For $m,d\\in\\mathbb N$, write $[m]:=\\{j\\in\\mathbb N:1\\le j\\le m\\}$, so $[0]=\\varnothing$, and give\n$$\\mathcal I_{m,d}:=\\{I\\subseteq[m]:|I|\\le d\\}$$\nits discrete sigma-algebra. For $d\\in\\mathbb N$, a **deterministic sample-compression scheme of size at most $d$** consists of a measurable compression map $\\kappa_m:\\mathcal Z^m\\to\\mathcal I_{m,d}$ for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$, and fixed reconstruction maps $G_k:\\mathcal Z^k\\times\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$ for $1\\le k\\le d$ and $G_0:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$, all measurable for the stated product and discrete sigma-algebras. If $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)$ and $I=\\kappa_m(S)=\\{i_1<\\cdots<i_k\\}$, write $S_I=(z_{i_1},\\ldots,z_{i_k})$ and define $g(S_I)(x):=G_k(S_I,x)$; for $I=\\varnothing$, use $G_0$. The scheme is **consistent** if, for every $c\\in\\mathcal H$, every $m\\in\\mathbb N$, and every $S=((x_1,c(x_1)),\\ldots,(x_m,c(x_m)))$, this reconstruction satisfies $g(S_I)(x_j)=c(x_j)$ for every $j\\in[m]$. The reconstruction map uses only the ordered selected labeled entries and no other side information; the reconstructed classifier need not belong to $\\mathcal H$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a supervised prediction problem with joint law $P$, the **population risk** of any measurable predictor $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$ (in particular, every $f\\in\\mathcal F$) is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(f):=\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}\\ell(f(x),y)\\,P(d(x,y))\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor an ordered sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_m,y_m))$ of length $m\\ge1$, the **empirical risk** or **training risk** of any such $f$ is\n$$\\widehat R_S(f):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(f(x_i),y_i)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nNeither definition assumes finite risk. The sum uses extended nonnegative arithmetic; since $m\\ge1$, any summand equal to $+\\infty$ makes the empirical risk $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-population-and-empirical-risk-are-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In a supervised prediction problem, for every measurable predictor $f$, the function $(x,y)\\mapsto\\ell(f(x),y)$ is measurable, so its population risk is a defined extended nonnegative integral. For every sample size $m\\ge1$, the empirical risk $S\\mapsto\\widehat R_S(f)$ is an extended nonnegative measurable function on the finite product sample space.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-compression-generalization-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma)$ be a measurable space, let $\\mathcal H$ be a nonempty class of measurable maps $c:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$, fix a target $c\\in\\mathcal H$, and let $D$ be a probability measure on $\\mathcal X$. Put $\\mathcal Z=\\mathcal X\\times\\{0,1\\}$ with the product sigma-algebra. Let $m,d\\in\\mathbb N$ satisfy $m>d$, set $[m]=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$, and draw $(X_1,\\ldots,X_m)$ under $D^m$, labeling $X_j$ by $c(X_j)$. Write $S=((X_1,c(X_1)),\\ldots,(X_m,c(X_m)))$. For each $0\\le i\\le d$, let $G_i:\\mathcal Z^i\\times\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$ be a fixed jointly measurable reconstruction map (for $i=0$, $G_0$ is measurable on $\\mathcal X$). For $I=\\{i_1<\\cdots<i_k\\}\\subseteq[m]$, define $S_I=((X_{i_1},c(X_{i_1})),\\ldots,(X_{i_k},c(X_{i_k})))$ and $g(S_I)(x):=G_k(S_I,x)$; for $I=\\varnothing$, let $S_I$ be the empty sequence and use $G_0$. Define $R_D(g(S_I)):=D\\{x:g(S_I)(x)\\ne c(x)\\}$. For every $\\epsilon\\in(0,1)$,\n$$\\Pr_{D^m}\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}\\text{there is an index subset }I\\subseteq[m],\\ |I|\\le d,\\text{ such that }R_D(g(S_I))>\\epsilon\\\\[-2pt]\\text{and }g(S_I)(X_j)=c(X_j)\\text{ for every }j\\in[m]\\end{array}\\right\\}\\le\\sum_{i=0}^{d}\\binom{m}{i}(1-\\epsilon)^{m-i}.$$\nThe event displayed above is measurable. Thus any measurable consistent compression learner of size at most $d$ using these reconstructors has failure probability no larger than this bound.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For the empty sample, the selected index set is empty and consistency is vacuous. For a positive-length sequence, the set of first-occurrence indices has at most two members, and the reconstruction maps are fixed functions of the selected entries. On a sample labeled by $c$, if an input $x$ occurs in the full sample, its first occurrence is selected and its selected label is $c(x)=x$; if it does not occur, no sample-consistency condition applies at $x$. Thus the reconstructed function agrees with every observed label, so the scheme is consistent in the sense of [F1]. All spaces are finite with their full sigma-algebras, so the compressor, reconstructor, risk, and output-risk event are measurable.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If at least one observation equals $1$, then the reconstruction returns $1$ at input $1$ and returns $0$ at input $0$, either from a selected entry or by the default rule, so its risk is $0$. If every observation is $0$, it returns $0$ at both inputs and errs only at $1$, so [F2] gives risk $D(\\{1\\})=1/2$. Independence and $D(\\{0\\})=1/2$ make the latter event have probability $(1/2)^m=2^{-m}$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
